<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Widukind</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 777–785</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Wittekind</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Widukind was a Westphalian nobleman who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a>. Taking refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> advanced, he returned to lead repeated revolts, including the victory at the Süntel in 782 that was followed by Charlemagne's massacre of Saxons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. In 785 he submitted and was baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attigny">Attigny</a> with Charlemagne as godfather, and later legend made him an ancestor of the Ottonian kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/widukind.jpg" alt="Widukind" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lucas Cranach the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nig_Widukind_von_Sachsen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little is known about Widukind, whose name literally translates as child of the forest. All sources about him come from his enemies, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, who painted him as an insurgent and a traitor, and his exact role in the campaigns is unknown. In the chronicles he is accompanied by Abbi, who may have been a close relative. The Royal Frankish Annals mention him first in 777, when he was the only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble not to appear at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>. He stayed instead with the Danish king Sigfred, possibly Sigurd Hring, whose sister Geva he had recently married.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 782 Widukind had returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and goaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobles into rebellion. From 782 to 784 Saxons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> fought annually, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> had 4,500 Saxons executed at the Massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a>. Widukind allied himself with the Frisians, but Charlemagne's winter attacks of 784 and 785 pushed him and his allies back to their homelands. In the Bardengau in 785 Widukind agreed to surrender in return for a guarantee that no bodily harm would be done to him. He and his allies were baptised, possibly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attigny">Attigny</a>, with Charlemagne as his godfather, and the peace brought acknowledgement of Saxon noble rank by the Frankish overlords.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>No contemporary source tells of his life or death after baptism. The historian Gerd Althoff assumed he was held in a monastery, perhaps Reichenau Abbey, but this is widely rejected. The Vita Liudgeri has him accompany <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> against the Veleti, and the 12th-century Kaiserchronik says Gerold of Baar killed him.</p><p>Legends made him a saintly builder of churches, and a Catholic Blessed with a feast day on 7 January. Since the 9th century he was treated as a mythical hero. The Ottonian kings claimed descent from him through Matilda, wife of King Henry I, as did the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-billung">House of Billung</a>. A tomb made around 1100 at Enger held the remains of a young woman, and three early 9th-century graves found in 1971 in front of the altar include a man assumed to be Widukind. German nationalists later made him a symbol, and after 1933 many plays were written about him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Widukind” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Saxons of northern Germany, between the Rhine and the Elbe, were conquered by Charlemagne in a series of brutal wars between 772 and 804, during which the Saxon leader <b>Widukind</b> submitted and was baptised in 785.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Saxon revolts followed whenever the Franks were engaged elsewhere, most famously under the Westphalian noble <b>Widukind</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Enger, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52,8&amp;year=781" aria-label="Westphalia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Widukind"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5420 5657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5420 5657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52,8&amp;year=781">What was here in 781 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
